 Newer / Updated Error-Handling Features
1. any_errors_fatal (Play-level)
- If set to true, any error on any host stops the entire play immediately.
- Useful for critical deployments where you don’t want partial success.
Example:
- hosts: webservers
  any_errors_fatal: true
  tasks:
    - name: Deploy app
      command: /usr/local/bin/deploy




2. max_fail_percentage
- Lets you tolerate a certain percentage of host failures before aborting the play.
- Great for large clusters where a few failures are acceptable.
Example:
- hosts: all
  max_fail_percentage: 20
  tasks:
    - name: Run update
      yum:
        name: myapp
        state: latest


👉 Play continues unless more than 20% of hosts fail.

3. ignore_unreachable (Inventory/Play-level)
- Newer option to ignore unreachable hosts instead of failing the play.
- Useful in dynamic cloud environments where some nodes may be temporarily offline.

4. Improved failed_when Logic
- In 2.20.x, evaluation is stricter and more predictable.
- You can now combine conditions with filters more safely.
Example:
- name: Check service health
  command: /usr/local/bin/healthcheck
  register: result
  failed_when:
    - result.rc != 0
    - "'CRITICAL' in result.stdout"


5. ignore_errors vs ignore_unreachable
- ignore_errors: skips task failures.
- ignore_unreachable: skips unreachable host failures.
- Both now produce clearer CLI messages so you know what was skipped.

6. Blocks with rescue + always
- Still the main structured error handler.
- In 2.20.x, error messages inside blocks are more detailed (task name, file, line).
- Easier to debug complex rescue logic.

